A||||27||frontal lobe||radio swap question||||1||
Z||000000||frontal lobe||09-27-2001||01:04 AM||||Does anyone know if a '98 Pontiac Grand Prix am/fm cassette stereo will fit right in the Fiero?  My cassette player doesn't work and the radio only does sometimes.  There is a radio on ebay I could bid on if it fits right in.  And your opinions on what the most you would pay for it?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||BlackARROW||09-27-2001||01:33 AM||||I would use this opportunity to ask if any radio specialist would know what the problem could be if the radio was to be dimmed at the point I have to put the volume to max in order to hear it clearly, but the cassette is allright?  Pretty anoying when you forgot to lower the volume before putting in the cassette  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I have checked with another antenna (with new cable), but it was not that?  Don't tell me it's inside and that I need a welder a pewter  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>THX||||reg||6
Z||000002||RossT||09-27-2001||01:34 AM||||Yes it will, but you will need the GM wire harness adapter. Read <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007662.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007662.html</A> <P>for more info.<BR> If you do not have the subwoofer, it is plug and play.  If you do have the fiero factor sub, then you need to make a couple of splices into the wire harness.||||reg||1
Z||000003||RossT||09-27-2001||01:42 AM||||Also check out <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20010824-2-008170.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20010824-2-008170.html</A> <P>for pics of the wire harness and where it goes.<P>The wire harness is a "universal" and some of the ends are not used (made for chrysler installs).<P>If "old lar" and I can do it, you can doo it too..<P>Make sure that your new/used pontiac deck has had the theft codes deactivated, or else when you power up you will need the code or have to take it to a dealer to have it unlocked.  TgOwens is the ultimate know all about this, is has been very helpfull..<p>[This message has been edited by RossT (edited 09-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||frontal lobe||09-27-2001||01:45 AM||||Thanks a lot, RossT.  I checked out the thread.  Lots of good info.  BTW, how are you doing, buddy?  Heard you got a taste of winter up your way already.||||reg||1
Z||000005||RossT||09-27-2001||01:48 AM||||Winter,!! It was about 80 today, but I know its on it's way.  I edited the above message so you may want to check it out. <P>Take care.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Old Lar||09-27-2001||05:23 AM||||The GP radio was an easy swap, once Terry told me of the "trick".  Looks good, sounds good.  I still should replace the rear speakers, but th CD works for this old tin eared gu.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||09-27-2001||09:59 AM||||For reconditioned Delco radio's and adapter harness' go to: <A HREF="http://mnrelectronics.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://mnrelectronics.com/</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero_hunter85||09-27-2001||03:57 PM||||why cant you just get the radio connector from the newer car and wire it in to the fiero?||||reg||1
Z||000009||mbramble||09-27-2001||04:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fiero_hunter85:<BR><B>why cant you just get the radio connector from the newer car and wire it in to the fiero?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>No reason at all.  If you have the 'donor car' radio connector, the only reason not to is so that your car can be returned to stock easily.  I guess that matters to some folks.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||RossT||10-05-2001||03:44 AM||||BUMP<P>Someone I know is trying to do this radio swap and is having problems.  He has the front radio surond piece off and the bolts holding the radio out, but the radio does not<BR>move forward.  Is that because the radio support in the back is still attached? If so how does one reach back there with the radio still installed.<P>Anyone??||||reg||1
Z||000011||frontal lobe||10-10-2001||12:46 AM||||Well, I pulled on the front of the radio so hard that I pulled the face plate off a little.  I found it much closer access from the driver's side than the passenger side, and reached the black "bullet" sticking through the bracket.  I pushed and pulled many times (I was afraid of breaking something) and finally the "bullet" came out and I got the radio out.<P>I had no problem hooking up the adapter, but have one question.  On the end that hooks into the Fiero wiring, the large plug was the only one I could find to hook up.  Are the other two pieces just not used?  Everything worked fine.<P>Now one final problem:  although it is a gm/delco am/fm cassette and fits perfectly in the slot, the mounting brackets on the side seem too wide and I can't get them past the edge of the console and up flush against the screw holes.  Any ideas??  Am I missing something?<P>I guess I could just leave it shoved in there unscrewed in and just leave the cover plate off...NOT!||||reg||1
Z||000012||Rick 88||10-10-2001||01:08 AM||||To avoid damaging your console vinyl do this.<BR>Trim off the left radio mounting tab to just past the screw holes. Do the same on the right also. You will need to elongate the upper slotted hole to mount the right side screw. The lower opening will not line up so you cant use it. <P>You may also need to bend the rear mounting tab inside the console back a little to get the radio to sit flush with the console mounts. <P>Rick 88||||reg||1
Z||000013||RossT||10-10-2001||02:49 AM||||You can file the sides down to make it fit better.  There is a 1999 article in FOCOA that did this very sway.  The said you just<BR>push the vinyl sides out and slip the mounting bracket in.  I personally would trim the sides as Rick suggested.  The other plugs are not used.  The are for Chrysler applications.  ||||reg||1
Z||000014||WampusCat||10-10-2001||01:11 PM||||This was part of the description of one of the stereos on Ebay: <BR>"Radio has GM's newer version of the Theftlock feature. This is *NOT* like the older systems where you can manually lock or unlock the radio by simply entering a code. The new system works with the serial data line on the vehicle's computer, and matches your car's VIN with that stored in the radio. Therefore, when you install this type of radio in your vehicle, it will initially be locked. Your local GM dealer or other auto repair shop with access to a GM "Scan Tool" will need to unlock the radio when it is installed in your vehicle. Once the radio has been unlocked by your dealer, you will be able to use it in your vehicle even if the battery gets disconnected for any reason, as the radio checks the VIN number each time it is turned on."<BR>Will this work in a Fiero?  Sounds like you need a pretty modern car to talk to the stereo.||||reg||1
Z||000015||frontal lobe||10-11-2001||09:17 PM||||Latest minor glitch.  Although the radio and cassette both work fine, the clock and presets don't hold their settings when I shut the ignition off.  Although this feature is great for calculating elapsed times to wherever I drive, it can be slightly annoying.<P>Did I do something wrong in setting it up??||||reg||1
Z||000016||WampusCat||10-11-2001||10:24 PM||||Sounds like you have both power leads hooked up to switched power.  There should be one lead that goes to a constant power source that is always on, even when key is not in.  That's for the clock and preset memory.||||reg||1
Z||000017||RossT||10-12-2001||12:00 AM||||Yes you need to connect one of the loose ends<BR>from your gm harness into your fiero's harness.<P>Look at the fourth pic; <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20010824-2-008170.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20010824-2-008170.html</A> <P>There are two loose ends at the bottom of the fourth pic.  On one of those ends you need to remove the plastic cover, it just snaps off, and plug whats left into your firo harness. That is one of your two needed power supplies, that leed will supply power all the time, even when the car is off. The other power supply is in the big black plug, and that only supplies power when the car is on.<P>so in conclusion, you need to connect the big black plug and the smaller 3 wire plug both into the fiero's harness (speperate spots) There is another 3 wire plug that is not used.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000018||frontal lobe||11-02-2001||01:51 AM||||I got my new (to me) 2000 pontiac cd radio and tried to put it in.  Question:  how do you get the blue plug in???  The clip is just TOO BIG to push it all the way.  Do you just push it on a little and leave it that way and it stays on?  Do you break off the top part and push it all the way in, and then it stays in?||||reg||1
Z||000019||frontal lobe||11-02-2001||01:54 AM||||And BTW, what does DIN stand for?  It's been bugging me.||||reg||1
Z||000020||RossT||11-02-2001||02:19 AM||||Old Lar e-mailed me on how to get the blue plug in.  When I get home I will let you know.  I think you use a flat blade screwdriver to slightly pry the frame open a bit, so the blue plug will slip in.  I don't think you have to remove the locking part of it to get it in.<P>I am doing mine this weekend, so wish me luck!!||||reg||1
Z||000021||crgkstnr||11-02-2001||02:31 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by frontal lobe:<BR><B>And BTW, what does DIN stand for?  It's been bugging me.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>DIN is "Deutsche Industrie Norm" or German Industry Standard.||||reg||1
Z||000022||frontal lobe||11-02-2001||02:34 AM||||Thanks, RossT.  And good luck this weekend.<P>I would have NEVER guessed that is what DIN meant.<P>Ooh, I just had a deja vu moment here, like I've done this before.  Those are wierd.||||reg||1
Z||000023||RossT||11-04-2001||12:05 AM||||Finally got mine in!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Image024_1.jpg"> <P>Was not as easy as I thought it was going to be. But it's in.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Old Lar||11-04-2001||12:08 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GPradio_1.jpg"> <P>Not as good of a photo as Ross', but you get te idea.||||reg||1
Z||000025||frontal lobe||11-05-2001||01:02 AM||||Hey, RossT, nice work!  Yeah, it seems like everything is harder than it should be.  Like the blue plug.  There's no way I was going to try to use a screwdriver to force room for the locking part of it that wouldn't fit.  My solution:  I just used a needle-nose pliers and pulled the stupid piece off. Fit nicely in after that.<P>I has a friend help me.  I crawled down on the driver's side and lifted the wiring harness OVER the bracket in the back while he pushed the radio in, then was able to fit the black nipple piece right in there.  Just pushed real hard and got the radio to seat fine.<P>I didn't even have to trim off the side brackets.  Was able to get it in there.  Did have to drill a hole on the left bracket between the top hole and the second hole.  Then one screw in each side and all snug and secure.<P>I'll have to post a pic when I figure out how to do it.  <P>I have the exact radio/cd as you do.  Looks great with the red back-lighting.  Very stock looking.<P>Now, about those old, dried out, sun-damaged crummy dash speakers...||||reg||1
Z||000026||frontal lobe||11-06-2001||04:35 PM||||Speaking of speakers, I took out the 4x10" dash speakers.  The foam around the perimeter was COMPLETELY dried out and cracked apart.  No wondering they were cracking and buzzing so much.  Stupid GM.  Can't even make speakers that sit exposed to the bright sun for the past 14 years without cracking all apart!<P>I don't need an awesome sound system to listen to the radio and my classical music cd's, so I just wanted some decent speakers that didn't buzz like mad.  Cheapest I could find were the pioneers and kenwoods for $99 at best buy, circuit city, etc., or on line for $80 plus about $10 shipping.<P>So my friend mentioned to try radio crap, I mean shack.  I NEVER think of them.  Went up to see if they had a couple 4x10's.  They did.  Closing them out.  $4.97 a speaker!  Put them in.  They are adequate, and they don't buzz.  Yes!||||reg||1
Z||000027||BN Boomer||11-06-2001||07:01 PM||||I put one in my 85 GT last year. I bought the adapter harness from Newell Electronics for $15 or so AND they helped me unlock the radio. Unfortunately, they have since been bought out by a bigger, less friendly company.<P>For whoever was thinking about splicing in the wiring harness from the donor car, trust, the adapter is cheap and well worth the money to save the hassle of figuring out which wire does what. Or maybe you just like unnecessary challenges.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> The adapter is a Delco part and can be bought or ordered through most GM dealers at minimal cost.<P>I also found that I had to use 1/4 inch thick nylon washers to bring the radio out to meet the trim plate.<P> <IMG SRC="http://wsphotofews.excite.com/009/ZO/jE/pZ/R497766.jpg"> ||||reg||1
